          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong infinitary algebraic theory
            is a variant of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            that permits operations of any (infinite) arity. As a motivating example, consider the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suplattices"} suplattices
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
            of
            = succeed "-many" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            Size issues can be tricky for infinitary algebraic theories. Naïvely, one might expect there to be an infinitary algebraic theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattices"} complete lattices
            too, but there is none, because a smallness condition fails. (This is connected to the nonexistence of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+complete+lattices"} free complete lattices
          %p
            (To explicitly distinguish between infinitary algebraic theories and algebraic theories, we may sometimes call the former
            = succeed ".)" do
              %strong finitary
          %p
            The notion of infinitary algebraic theory is distinguished here from the concept of
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
              \-ary algebraic theory
            for an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arity+class"} arity class
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A
            %strong many-sorted infinitary algebraic theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            with all small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            and with a small collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            of objects (called
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong sorts
          %p
            Given a small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              \-sorted infinitary algebraic theory
            is a locally small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            with all small products and equipped with an
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            of objects (called
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong sorts
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            on
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℛ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{D},\mathcal{R})
            is an
            = succeed "-sorted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            is a many-sorted infinitary algebraic theory; conversely, any many-sorted infinitary algebraic theory may be interpreted as an
            = succeed "-sorted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is (the set of isomoprhism classes of) an appropriate family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            and the
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            An
            %strong unsorted infinitary algebraic theory
            is a locally small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            with all small products and equipped with an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            (so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{D},R)
            is a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+category"} pointed category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^n
            for some small cardinal number
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %strong infinitary algebraic theory
            is by default an unsorted infinitary algebraic theory, invoking the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
            = succeed "-sorted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p We will give the definitions of further special cases for unsorted infinitary algebraic theories, but these definitions may also be generalised to the many-sorted case.
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+cardinal"} regular cardinal
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
              \-ary algebraic theory
            is a locally small pointed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{D},R)
            with all
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \lt \kappa
            such that every object is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^n
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \lt \kappa
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \leq \lambda
          %p
            A
            %strong bounded infinitary algebraic theory
            is an infinitary algebraic theory which is (under the interpretation above)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to some
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            A
            %strong finitary algebraic theory
            is a locally small pointed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{D},R)
            with all finitary products such that every object is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^n
            for some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            is by default a finitary algebraic theory, invoking the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_0
          %h2#size_matters Size matters
          %p
            Given a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            with small products, we may be interested in knowing whether it is equivalent to a many-sorted infinitary algebraic theory (with a set of sorts).
            %a{:href => "http://tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/1995/n9/1-09abs.html"} Freyd and Street (1995)
            have shown that a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            if and only if both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{D},Set]
            are locally small. Analogously, it seems that a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            with products may be given the structure of a many-sorted infinitary algebraic theory (with a set of sorts) if and only if both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prod[\mathcal{D},Set]
            (the category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product-preserving+functor"} product-preserving functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{D},Set]
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/comments.php?DiscussionID=1673"} the n-Forum
            for more preliminary results.
          %h2#algebraic_categories Algebraic Categories
          .un_lemma
            %h6#conjecture Conjecture
            %p
              A multi-sorted infinitary algebraic theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              defines a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+category"} monadic category
              over
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              by taking the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
              consisting of all
              %strong product-preserving
              covariant functors from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              in to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            Let us write this functor category as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prod[\mathcal{D},Set]
            and start by showing that it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
          #ProdLocSmall.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prod[\mathcal{D},Set]
              of product-preserving functors and natural transformations is locally small.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left:2em;"}
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1, V_2 \colon \mathcal{D} \to Set
              be two product-preserving covariant functors. From the definition of a multi-sorted infinitary category we see that we may choose a set of sorts, say
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_s \in \mathcal{D}
              be the image of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S
            %p
              As
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is a set, the product
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\prod_{s \in S} Set(V_1(D_s), V_2(D_s))
            %p
              is a set. We define a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [V_1,V_2] \to \prod_{s \in S} Set(V_1(D_s), V_2(D_s))
              by sending a natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \colon V_1 \to V_2
              to the product of its values at each
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_s
            #eq:natrans.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi D
                          %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\alpha \mapsto \big(\alpha_{D_s}\big)_{s \in S}.
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \in \mathcal{D}
              be an arbitrary object. From the definition of a multi-sorted infinitary algebraic theory, there is an isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi D
                    %mo ≅
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d \colon D \cong \prod_{s \in S} D_s^{X_s}
            %p
              for some sets
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_s
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X_s
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi S
                      %msubsup
                        %mi D
                        %mi s
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi s
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_{s,x} \colon \prod_{s \in S} D_s^{X_s} \to D_s
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2
              are product-preserving, we have isomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≅
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\cong  \prod_{s \in S} V_i(D_s)^{X_s}
            %p
              commuting with the projections. Thus for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X_s
            %svg{:height => "229", "se:nonce" => "37480", :width => "655", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com"}
              %g
                %title Layer 1
                %foreignobject#svg_37480_1{"font-size" => "16", :height => "30", :width => "110.000001", :x => "175", :y => "48"}
                  %math{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
